## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of low-level memory management, per-thread caching strategies, lock-free data structures, memory ordering semantics, and NUMA awareness in practical allocator design.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-size block allocators
- Thread-local storage (TLS) freelists with global slab pool
- Lock-free freelist with ABA avoidance using atomic compare-exchange
- Memory ordering: acquire/release semantics
- NUMA locality considerations and fragmentation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: alloc/free for fixed-size blocks
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; } Block;
Block* tls_freelist_head;
Block* global_pool_pull();

Block* alloc() {
  Block* b = tls_freelist_head;
  if (!b) {
    b = global_pool_pull();
    if (!b) return NULL; // OOM
  }
  tls_freelist_head = b->next;
  return b;
}

void free(Block* b) {
  b->next = tls_freelist_head;
  tls_freelist_head = b;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Low-level memory management on constrained hardware.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-size allocator (slab)
- Free-list stored inside blocks; index-based chaining
- O(1) allocations/deallocations with minimal metadata
- Alignment, memory overhead, and fragmentation considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct {
  uint32_t next; // index of next free slot
} FreeSlot;

#define POOL_SIZE 256
#define SLOT_SIZE 64
static uint8_t pool[POOL_SIZE * SLOT_SIZE];
static uint32_t head = 0; // index of first free slot
static uint32_t used = 0;

void slab_init(void) {
  for (uint32_t i = 0; i < POOL_SIZE - 1; ++i) {
    ((uint32_t*)pool)[i] = i + 1;
  }
  ((uint32_t*)pool)[POOL_SIZE - 1] = UINT32_MAX;
  head = 0;
  used = 0;
}

void* slab_alloc(void) {
  if (head == UINT32_MAX) return NULL;
  uint32_t idx = head;
  head = ((uint32_t*)pool)[idx];
  ++used;
  return pool + idx * SLOT_SIZE;
}

void slab_free(void* p) {
  ptrdiff_t idx = ((uint8_t*)p - pool) / SLOT_SIZE;
  ((uint32_t*)pool)[idx] = head;
  head = (uint32_t)idx;
  --used;
}
```

